# Axon hardware development environment

Axon is a monorepo for RTL, reusable IP, simulators, and the tools that build
and verify them. FuseSoC core files are the authoritative HDL composition and
dependency graph. Repository-owned FuseSoC and Edalize sources provide a
repeatable environment for designers, automation, and AI coding agents.

## Quick start

The host currently needs Git, Make, Python 3.10 or newer, GCC/G++, and
Verilator. Bootstrap initially needs network access for pinned Python packages.

```bash
git clone <repository-url>
cd <repository-directory>
python3 tools/env/bootstrap.py
python3 tools/env/doctor.py
tools/bin/fusesoc core list
```

Bootstrap creates an ignored `.venv` and installs the Axon-owned FuseSoC and
Edalize packages directly from this checkout. Shell activation is unnecessary,
and rerunning bootstrap is safe. Doctor reports host tools and resolved local
packages without changing the environment. Formal users install the pinned,
repo-local open-source tool bundle separately:

```bash
python3 tools/env/formal_setup.py
```

## Current IP catalog

| Core | Purpose | Supported targets |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `axon:core:riscv:0.1.0` | RV32IM processor and prebuilt I/M instruction tests | `default`, `lint`, `sim`, `sim_fw`, `coverage` |
| `axon:bus:components:0.1.0` | APB and AXI4-Lite components | `default`, `lint` |
| `axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0` | Single-iteration iSLIP allocator | `default`, `lint`, `sim`, `coverage`, `formal`, `formal_cover` |
| `axon:pe:syst:0.1.0` | Dual-buffered 2x2 prototype; matrix simulation currently fails | `default`, `lint`, `sim`, `sim_fifo` |
| `axon:prim:arbiter:0.1.0` | Fixed, PPC, and tree arbiters | `default`, qualified lint targets |
| `axon:prim:arbiter_rr_select:0.1.0` | Stateless round-robin selector | `default`, `lint` |
| `axon:prim:fifo:0.1.0` | Synchronous and asynchronous FIFO family | `default`, qualified lint targets |
| `axon:prim:slice:0.1.0` | Two-entry ready/valid slice | `default`, `lint` |
| `axon:prim:sum_tree:0.1.0` | Parallel combinational sum tree | `default`, `lint` |
| `axon:prim:sync_reqack:0.1.0` | Request/acknowledge CDC | `default`, `lint` |
| `axon:prim:assert:0.1.0` | Portable assertion macros | `default` |
| `axon:prim:cdc_rand_delay:0.1.0` | DV-only randomized CDC delay | `default` |
| `axon:prim:count:0.1.0` | Configurable saturating counter | `default` |
| `axon:prim:flop:0.1.0` | Generic flop primitives | `default` |
| `axon:prim:flop_2sync:0.1.0` | Flop-based synchronizer | `default` |
| `axon:prim:util:0.1.0` | Shared RTL utilities | `default` |

`axon:lint:common:0.1.0` provides shared Verilator configuration rather than
synthesizable IP.

## Dependency model

Consumers depend on the narrowest reusable core and receive only its `default`
RTL fileset. Testbenches, formal harnesses, coverage monitors, and lint waivers
do not leak through dependencies. The first verification slice resolves as:

```text
axon:noc:islip_arbiter
  -> axon:prim:arbiter_rr_select
     -> axon:prim:util
        -> axon:prim:assert
```

Inspect the authoritative catalog and an individual core with:

```bash
tools/bin/fusesoc core list
tools/bin/fusesoc core show axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0
```

## Basic FuseSoC commands

Lint iSLIP:

```bash
tools/bin/fusesoc run --target lint axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0
```

Run a self-checking simulation:

```bash
tools/bin/fusesoc run --target sim axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0 \
  --TEST=directed --SEED=1 --CYCLES=200
```

Run one coverage-instrumented simulation:

```bash
tools/bin/fusesoc run --target coverage axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0 \
  --TEST=random --SEED=42 --CYCLES=200
```

The direct coverage target writes one raw database to
`build/axon_noc_islip_arbiter_0.1.0/coverage/coverage.dat`.

Prove the iSLIP safety properties and generate cover witnesses:

```bash
tools/bin/fusesoc run --target formal axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0
tools/bin/fusesoc run --target formal_cover axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0
```

Formal results live under the corresponding `formal-symbiyosys/build/` and
`formal_cover-symbiyosys/build/` directories beneath the core's build root.
Failed proofs and successful cover searches include VCD traces that can be
opened with GTKWave.

## Integrated validation

The primary RISC-V health and coverage commands are now:

```sh
tools/bin/fusesoc regress axon:core:riscv:0.1.0 --suite health
tools/bin/fusesoc coverage axon:core:riscv:0.1.0 --suite coverage
tools/bin/fusesoc status axon:core:riscv:0.1.0
```

Health runs 97 declared checks, using one simulator build for all firmware runs.
Reports include HTML, Markdown, JSON and JUnit; failures retain logs/waves and
verified replay inputs. Core-local declarations replace ad hoc campaign command
assembly. Read [the validation guide](docs/validation.md) for the DVSim-style
model, test selection, replay, coverage interpretation and migration. Earlier
Make/Python interfaces remain compatibility paths. Dependency-selected smoke checks are available with
`tools/bin/fusesoc presubmit --base <reference> --dry-run`; remove `--dry-run`
to run the affected cores and their consumers. Missing smoke declarations block
the gate. Eligible simulator builds now use a verified local content-addressed
cache; `fusesoc cache list` explains retained entries. Additional cores need
qualified smoke suites, and CI enforcement is not configured.

## Legacy Make/Python orchestration

The root Makefile and Python runners remain available for compatibility. They
contain no RTL file lists and call FuseSoC for dependency resolution and builds.
Use the integrated FuseSoC commands above for new test/regression/coverage work.

```bash
# Environment convenience
make bootstrap
make doctor
make cores

# Retained lint result
make lint CORE=axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0

# Build once and run deterministic random seeds concurrently
make sim-regress CORE=axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0 \
  TEST=random SEED_START=1 SEED_COUNT=100 JOBS=8 CYCLES=200

# Instrument, run a campaign, merge, and report categorized coverage
make coverage CORE=axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0 \
  SEED_START=1 SEED_COUNT=100 JOBS=8 CYCLES=200
```

Disposable compilation output lives under `build/`. Unique simulation,
regression, and coverage evidence lives under `artifacts/`. Coverage campaigns
produce per-run databases plus `merged.dat`, `summary.txt`, `summary.json`,
`coverage.info`, annotated RTL, logs, and a compatibility manifest.

## Repository map

```text
ip/                     RTL, DV collateral, and FuseSoC cores
models/                 architectural/performance models
platforms/              integration manifests
sw/                     firmware source and platform support
verification/           shared verification integrations
prebuilt/               executable tests and provenance
third_party/            pinned external dependency identities
scripts/regtool/        register-description and RTL-generation tools
scripts/coverage/       reusable coverage reporting tools
scripts/fusesoc/        Axon-maintained FuseSoC source
scripts/edalize/        Axon-maintained Edalize source
tools/env/              deterministic setup and diagnosis
tools/build/            legacy execution wrappers
docs/                   tracked development documentation
.agents/skills/         repository-local AI workflows
build/                  disposable generated builds
artifacts/              retained run evidence and reports
```

## Development manual

The [Axon hardware development manual](docs/development-manual.md) defines the
coding, FuseSoC, verification, generation, documentation, and AI-assisted
review principles. Designers and agents should read it before changing
supported RTL or tooling. Commands marked legacy document compatibility interfaces. New campaign
features belong in the integrated FuseSoC command family.

## RISC-V firmware verification

See [the core guide](ip/core/README.md) for RTL lint, ELF simulation and the
prebuilt 47-test ACT4 I/M collection. ACT4/Sail/compiler setup is isolated in a
separate generation workspace; ordinary test execution uses Verilator and the
repository bootstrap only. Full privileged compliance is not claimed.

## Repository navigation

[AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) maps specifications, commands and source ownership for
AI-assisted work. [Repository layout](docs/repository-layout.md) describes the
shared CPU/platform/firmware boundaries. The customized BookSim model now lives
in [models/noc](models/noc/README.md). The reproducible architectural-test recipe
is in [verification/riscv_arch](verification/riscv_arch/README.md); installed tools
remain optional and external.

Legacy retained validation (Make/Python CLI)
------------------------------------------
These compatibility entry points retain evidence for individual targets and
composed per-IP checks.
They use the same FuseSoC cores as direct commands:

    python3 tools/build/check_ip.py --core axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0
    python3 tools/build/check_ip.py --core axon:pe:syst:0.1.0

Equivalent temporary shorthand: make check-ip CORE=<full-vlnv>.
Defaults: 100 random seeds, 8 workers, 200 cycles, plus directed/lint and the
profile's formal/local checks. Profiles live in verification/ip_checks.json.
The syst top-level simulation currently exposes a matrix mismatch; its gate
must fail until the behavioral repair is agreed and verified.

Retained individual run, including failure waves and a JSON manifest:

    python3 tools/build/run_target.py sim --core axon:pe:syst:0.1.0 --target sim_fifo --test directed --seed 1 --cycles 2000
    python3 tools/build/run_target.py formal --core axon:noc:islip_arbiter:0.1.0 --target formal

Each result prints its artifact path and exact rerun command. --timeout,
--max-log-mib and --max-wave-mib bound target execution. Passing simulations
remove waves; failures retain them under artifacts/. Direct FuseSoC remains
usable but does not itself provide the composed gate/retained manifest contract.
